There are no trials yet. Across four seeds the starting architecture scored either 0.068 or 0.258, so seed luck alone swings results by about 0.19. Only a large or repeated gain should count.

The default recipe gets 131k steps in batches of 4096, which is only 32 policy updates. That is very few, so update frequency may matter more than network size. To place a brick correctly, the agent must (a) see its landing layer, which is the highest filled cell under the footprint, and (b) check that the target needs every footprint cell at that layer. The starting network uses a 1x1 head at the brick's anchor cell, so it has to learn both steps implicitly.

This round runs three separate tests:
1. a recipe-only change to more frequent updates;
2. the same network with a head that takes max, min and mean over each footprint, which matches how bricks land;
3. a new encoder whose shared 2D convolutions see each layer next to the layers above and below it.
